{
  "dom_id": "step-q361829",
  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14222582/assets/778041d84c40044d6f047203a017bd64c0569bcfc9442afca9baa5160b494ae7.png)\n\nThe region shown above enclosed by the curves $y=\\sqrt{x}+1$ and $y=\\frac{x}{2}+1$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $y=-1.$ Find the volume $V$ of the resulting solid. Do not round the answer.\n\n$V=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "First, we want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{x}+1 & =\\frac{x}{2}+1 \\\\ \\sqrt{x} & =\\frac{x}{2} \\\\ 2\\sqrt{x} & =x \\\\ x & =0,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, our limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=4.$\n\nNow, let's draw our vertical arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14222582/assets/7ce02bca7667a9c32971b7840ff2ba4b96ef3a5c9b18aceaeb5b9b713cc7b36f.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $y=\\frac{x}{2}+1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $y=\\sqrt{x}+1.$\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $y=\\sqrt{x}+1.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14222582/assets/6cb557a57a189a426b6cccacd599562332b05aace5bbbfc5d996b0d53fed3359.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}[\\,(\\sqrt{x}+1)-(-1)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}{[\\sqrt{x}+2]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $y=\\frac{x}{2}+1.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14222582/assets/26886afd61251cae273c086dba59dcfa912665cdb788b0c3ef866bf61907efa2.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}[\\,(\\frac{x}{2}+1)-(-1)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}{[\\frac{x}{2}+2]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}{[\\sqrt{x}+2]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}{[\\frac{x}{2}+2]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}({(\\sqrt{x}+2)}^{2}-{(\\frac{x}{2}+2)}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}(-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-x+4\\sqrt{x})\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {(-\\frac{1}{12}\\,{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}\\,{x}^{2}+\\frac{8}{3}\\,{x}^{3/2})|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =\\pi ((-\\frac{1}{12}\\,(4)^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}\\,(4)^{2}+\\frac{8}{3}\\,(4)^{3/2})-(0)) \\\\  & =\\pi (8) \\\\  & =8\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
